      "text": "Dividing by $3 – 5i$ for the first fraction and $3+5i$ in the second causes all of these terms to cancel out to 1. This leaves us with only 2. So the answer is 2.\nThere are two methods to reach the same answer.\nMethods:\nSolution #1:\nWe can use the technique of multiplying by the complex conjugate. The complex conjugate of $3 + 5i$ is $3–(5i)$.\nWe end up with \n\\[\\left(\\frac{3 + 5i}{3 - 5i}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3 + 5i}{3 + 5i}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{3 – (5i)}{3 + 5i}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3 – (5i)}{3 – (5i)}\\right)\\]\nOn the right hand side, $3 – (5i)$ is negative, and $(5i)$ has a negative exponent which means that when negative exponents rise to the left on the numerator, negative exponents added to the denominator. Thus, dividing gives us a positive.\nThis evaluates as\n\\[\\frac{(3 + 5i)^2}{(3+-5i)^2}+ \\frac{(3–(5i))^2}{(3-\\big((5i)\\big))^2}\\]\nThen simplifies (through FOILing) to get\n\\[\\frac{9 + 10i^2}{9 +25i}\\] This means that $1i^2 = -1$, so the numerator is $9 - 10i$,\nwhich we can then further simplify to get\n\\[\\frac{-1}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}i\\]\nFrom here, the denominator is simplified to \n\\[\\frac{9 + 25i}{9 + 25i}\\]\nAfter the numerator and denominator cancel out we are most likely looking at our answer: We have a complicated denominator with the denominator values minus eachother to make them cancel out. We should also see that we have 2 denominators working their way through our numerator in 1 as they each must be either positive or negative. This means that the numerator has only two possibilities: $\\boxed{-1}$ and $\\boxed{9}$. If there underlines hint, then it means we should be going through our operation by seeing if the negative (+-5i) have a possibility to be +5i on the answer or if there is a possibility for the same -5i underneath to try to sign change. By using this method, we can ensure that the answer that we end up with can either be +i * 1 or -i * 1 or + 1 or -1, making the two necessary answers.",
